temporal (seasons to millions of years) and spatial (molecular to global) scales. An over-arching aspiration of this research is to understand the processes and feedbacks operating in the Earth System and how they relate to global changes in climate and tectonics. In addition, I am\u2026","keywords":["Biogeochemistry"],"links":[{"label":"Lab Web Site","url":"https://paytanlab.ucsc.edu/"}],"photo":{"credit":"VRT","license":"CC BY-SA 3.0","source_url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Adina_2022.jpg","thumb_url":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/24/Adina_2022.jpg/250px-Adina_2022.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail"}},"schema_version":"s3.1","works":[{"abstract":"","author_count":4,"author_ids":["A5056834092","A5089742767","A5093426005","A5096004377"],"author_institution_ids":[],"authors":[{"id":"A5093426005","name":"Lauren Hashman"},{"id":"A5089742767","name":"Adina Paytan"},{"id":"A5096004377","name":"Kheira Boy"},{"id":"A5056834092","name":"Eyal Rahav"}],"ca_any_author":true,"ca_led":false,"cited_by_count":0,"doi":"https://doi.org/10.1130/abs/2023am-395021","domain_tags":["air","soil_and_agriculture"],"facet_tags":["matrix:air","matrix:marine","matrix:soil_and_agriculture"],"institution_ids":["I185103710","I4210154993"],"is_oa":false,"journal":"Abstracts with programs - Geological Society of America","last_updated":"2026-09-27T08:07:27.782135","license":"","needs_review":false,"oa_status":"closed","oa_url":"https://doi.org/10.1130/abs/2023am-395021","openalex_topics":["T10419","T10753","T12383"],"plastic_related":true,"plastic_tier":"core","primary_topic":"T10753","pub_date":"2023-01-01","study_type":"conference-paper","title":"COASTAL VERSUS TERRESTRIAL DEPOSITION OF ATMOSPHERIC MICROPLASTICS IN CALIFORNIA","type":"conference-paper","work_id":"W4389358143","year":2023},{"abstract":"Nile Red is widely used for the detection of microplastics because its fluorescence emission is sensitive to local polarity and can distinguish hydrophobic plastics from hydrophilic ones. The fluorescence of the molecular rotor, 9-(dicyanovinyl)-julolidine (DCVJ) is less sensitive to polarity but more to viscosity. DCVJ is less widely used for microplastic analysis, although it has been used to detect polystyrene nanobeads. Here, we compared these dyes with standard samples from the Hawaii Pacific University Polymer Kit 1.0 and confirmed that Nile Red, in general, was better for the detection and identification of microplastics. Fluorescence emission was analyzed using photography, as well as spectroscopy. The color and peak emission wavelength of some stained environmental microplastics were affected by additives. Raman spectroscopy was used to confirm the chemical identity of such samples. Although DCVJ emits green fluorescence on binding to some microplastics, a peak at 620 nm has been reported with polystyrene nanobeads, attributed to dimer/excimer formation. We confirmed this property and directly observed diffraction-limited spots using fluorescence microscopy, attributed to single or just a few nanobeads. Nile Red also stains polystyrene nanobeads and gave stronger signals than with DCVJ, but Nile Red was prone to false positives due to dye aggregation in aqueous solutions.","author_count":7,"author_ids":["A5010961545","A5024757046","A5026393571","A5039831019","A5057416311","A5089742767","A5146084459"],"author_institution_ids":["I185103710"],"authors":[{"id":"A5057416311","name":"Madison Wallner"},{"id":"A5146084459","name":"Justin Diaz"},{"id":"A5010961545","name":"Amelia B. Labbe"},{"id":"A5026393571","name":"James J. Jacob"},{"id":"A5039831019","name":"Quentin Williams"},{"id":"A5089742767","name":"Adina Paytan"},{"id":"A5024757046","name":"Clive R. Bagshaw"}],"ca_any_author":true,"ca_led":true,"cited_by_count":0,"doi":"https://doi.org/10.64898/2026.08.03.742549","domain_tags":["analytical_methods"],"facet_tags":[],"institution_ids":["I185103710","I4210111358"],"is_oa":true,"journal":"bioRxiv (Cold Spring Harbor Laboratory)","last_updated":"2026-11-09T13:22:03.289127","license":"cc-by-nc-nd","needs_review":false,"oa_status":"green","oa_url":"https://www.biorxiv.org/content/biorxiv/early/2026/08/07/2026.08.03.742549.full.pdf","openalex_topics":["T10540","T10753","T11240"],"plastic_related":true,"plastic_tier":"core","primary_topic":"T10753","pub_date":"2026-08-07","study_type":"preprint","title":"The comparative strengths and limitations of Nile Red and 9-(dicyanovinyl)-julolidine (DCVJ) fluorescent dyes for detecting microplastics and nanoplastics","type":"preprint","work_id":"W7202004571","year":2026}]}
